From be361b4ad57f1b61ad5ee5d3559569c6cdde0caf Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: flippy <flippy@thebe.tail087954.ts.net>
Date: Tue, 29 Sep 2026 20:19:01 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] Streaming tile: turn the AirPlay receiver on and off

The panel had no way to reach the receiver at all -- it was enabled by
setting a system property by hand over adb, which is not something a
viewer can do. The tile shows what the box is advertising as a streaming
target and offers a switch per target.

Toggling one starts or stops its *service* rather than writing the
property here, even though this package is allowed to write it. The
property is only what init watches to run the daemon; the service is
what holds audio focus and turns the daemon's metadata into a
MediaSession. A daemon started without it would play over whatever else
is on and put nothing on screen, so the two have to move together. The
service sets the property once it is up, which is why the property is
still what the tile reads back.

Three details worth keeping:

- The receivers are separate packages, so the tile resolves each
  service before offering it and hides itself when none is installed.
  That keeps this fork buildable on a tree without them.

- startService, not startForegroundService. The panel is on screen, so
  the background-start restriction does not apply, and
  startForegroundService is actively wrong for the stop case: it
  promises a startForeground() that a service shutting itself down
  never makes, and the platform kills it for the broken promise.

- The tile is re-read in onResume, not only when the panel opens. The
  receiver can also stop from outside the panel -- losing audio focus
  does it -- and the summary would otherwise go stale.

The second slot in the row is left empty for the video receiver.

